  mike gerlay has his story  it's not easy to find the final resting  spot of one of the most decorated  indigenous soldiers in canadian history  francis pekin bagabo the sniper would  have likely wanted it that way  where there is no doubt is that he would  have found this quiet corner of the  wassoxing first nation near perry sound  peaceful free from the horrors of war  and politics of a country that all but  forgot what he did i would say his story  may have been sleeping it was a story  that was you know told um in  quiet  settings uh within his family or from  amongst community members he was known  as peggy to his fellow soldiers and a  terror to the enemy during some of the  most horrific battles of the first world  war some iprae passiondale he would  sneak into no man's land and wait his  patience led him to being credited with  378 kills and over 300 enemy captured  and after four years on the front lines  he was discharged with  68 898.68 in pay  he was happy to return to the shores of  georgian bay but like most indigenous  veterans he had hoped for a newfound  respect  instead it was more of the same poverty  and persecution  and the country he fought and bled for  still wouldn't grant him citizenship  they were trying to prove themselves  as equals  not to assimilate but to prove  themselves as equals as indigenous men  as indigenous canadians pekomagabo never  did stop fighting working tirelessly to  get more autonomy for band councils  until he died in 1952 he dedicated his  life fighting for indigenous rights of  course he returned a decorated military  hero but it was that work he did at  community levels at organizational  levels at uh you know being a warrior  for the rights for the language for the  culture of his people those are the  active contributions that i think we  most  know him for  okiewich  in was soxing today the name francis  pagamagabo is revered in both ojibwe and  english he was one of 39 canadian  soldiers awarded the military medal and  two bars for bravery  but it took until 2016 before he was  immortalized in bronze strong and  defiant on the shores of georgian bay  a hero given his due  mike trolley global news parry sound  ontario  no stone left alone next how younger  canadians connect with the stories of  fallen soldiers  there are many ways canadians can  reflect on those who served and  sacrificed for our country the no stone  left alone initiative is one of them  students place poppies on the headstones  of veterans connecting them to the  stories of the fallen it's a simple  gesture but an important and meaningful  one ross lord visited one special event  in the small town of middleton nova  scotia  [Music]  their faces are covered but their hearts  are open  students from nova scotia's middleton  regional high school paying tribute to  airmen who died during training missions  in the second world war at nearby cfb  greenwood the greenwood had  56 crashes but 60 65 men commonwealth  men were killed  and of  those men 24 are buried here for many of  these students like air cadet connor  obishon laying poppies at headstones  comes naturally albert j bartra royal  air force  my family has been primarily infantry  henley maurice albert william pilot  officer navigator royal air force  volunteer reserve  was just kind of thinking about like the  people that are under the earth and how  they  were so determined to just  protect our country to the point where  it cost them their life  [Music]  these tributes are coordinated by the no  stone left alone memorial foundation  started by this edmonton family they lay  poppies every year to honor family  members who served in the military  looking out at these vast amount of  headstones i wondered why is it only our  grandparents like why do we stop here  and we place it only on them ten years  later no stone left alone has grown into  a national symbol of gratitude pretty  soon word got out  and it continued to grow from there and  so this year  you know it warms my heart we have a  hundred communities even during this  covid half the people these days don't  even think about remembrance day anymore  they just think it's something that they  have to sit through an hour long and  just  they don't get to go on their phones but  i think it's nice to respect and honor  all the people who have sacrificed their  lives because we wouldn't be here today  if it wasn't for them  even a small gesture can have big  meaning just ask relatives of pilot  officer trevor pithock he was training  to be a navigator when his plane flew  into a fog shrouded hill in 1943. it's  sort of connects me in a in a funny way  i mean i don't know the students i don't  know who's doing it but it does it is  comforting to know that somebody is you  know he's being remembered and somebody  is is making that connection  venerable poppy incapable new hands  ross lord global news  middleton nova scotia  and that is global national for this  thursday remembrance day i'm donna  friesen we leave you with images from  the tomb of the unknown soldier in  ottawa where it's become a tradition for  people to come and lay their poppies  after the national ceremony  thanks for watching take care of  yourselves  bye bye  [Music]  down  [Music]  you
 

